* AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\nL100:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features】\nL101:       * 【57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features】\nL102:       * 【58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support】\nL103: \nL104:   * 【59†Pricing】\nL105: \nL106: Search`/`\nL107: \nL108: 【1†Sign in】\nL109: \nL110: 【60†Sign up】\nL111: \nL112: Appearance settings\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†THU-HAS】\nL116:   2. 【62†MegaZK】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2609.36437] Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36437)\nciteturn1view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36437\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 Sep 2026]\nL9: # Title:Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Taeung Yoon】, 【6†Yupeng Zhang】, 【7†Xiaojing Liao】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference, by Taeung Yoon and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Zero-knowledge proofs are emerging as a promising approach for enabling private, verifiable LLM governance and auditing, where regulators, users, and auditors need to verify claims about training-data usage or LLM inference-time behavior, while model providers must protect proprietary model parameters. However, despite the growing interest in ZK-LLMs, the understanding of ZK-friendly quantization remains limited.\nL17: This gap matters because in the ZK setting, quantization directly shapes the arithmetic structure, constraint complexity, and proving cost of ZK inference. ZK protocols operate over finite fields and incur costs that depend heavily on the number and type of arithmetic operations, nonlinearities, and lookup constraints. Understanding ZK-friendly quantization is therefore essential for making ZK-LLMs practical. In this work, we present the first systematic study of ZK-friendly quantization for LLMs.\nL18: We first formalize the definition of ZK-friendly quantization, capturing the properties required for ZK proof generation. We then evaluate nine language models, including Qwen2.5-14B and the mixture-of-experts model Qwen3-30B-A3B, across a broad design space of weight, activation, and nonlinear lookup table precision. Our results show that activation precision is substantially more sensitive than weight precision, while nonlinear lookup approximations can become the dominant source of utility degradation.\nL19: Also, we identify RMSNorm inverse-square-root lookups as a recurring bottleneck in several large models and recover near-baseline utility by selectively increasing precision only at the bottleneck. Finally, we show that reducing bit-width or lookup-table size does not necessarily yield proportional end-to-end proving savings, showing that conventional low-bit quantization heuristics do not directly translate to ZK proving efficiency and motivating operator-aware precision selection.\nL20: Comments:  | 22 pages, 5 figures\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:2609.36437】 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:2609.36437v1】 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.36437†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Taeung Yoon [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Tue, 29 Sep 2026 00:54:35 UTC (204 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference, by Taeung Yoon and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【16†Audio Summary】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScalable Zero-knowledge Proofs for Non-linear Functions in Machine Learning (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/507)\nciteturn1view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/507\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 79\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2025/507\nL30: ### 【17†Scalable Zero-knowledge Proofs for Non-linear Functions in Machine Learning】\nL31: \nL32: Meng Hao, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China\nL33: \nL34: Hanxiao Chen, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China\nL35: \nL36: Hongwei Li, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China\nL37: \nL38: Chenkai Weng, Northwestern University\nL39: \nL40: Yuan Zhang, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China\nL41: \nL42: Haomiao Yang, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China\nL43: Tianwei Zhang, Nanyang Technological University\nL44: ##### Abstract\nL45: Zero-knowledge (ZK) proofs have been recently explored for the integrity of machine learning (ML) inference. However, these protocols suffer from high computational overhead, with the primary bottleneck stemming from the evaluation of non-linear functions. In this paper, we propose the first systematic ZK proof framework for non-linear mathematical functions in ML using the perspective of table lookup.\nL46: The key challenge is that table lookup cannot be directly applied to non-linear functions in ML since it would suffer from inefficiencies due to the intolerably large table. Therefore, we carefully design several important building blocks, including digital decomposition, comparison, and truncation, such that they can effectively utilize table lookup with a quite small table size while ensuring the soundness of proofs.\nL47: Based on these blocks, we implement complex mathematical operations and further construct ZK proofs for current mainstream non-linear functions in ML such as ReLU, sigmoid, and normalization. The extensive experimental evaluation shows that our framework achieves 50 ∼ 179× runtime improvement compared to the state-of-the-art work, while maintaining a similar level of communication efficiency.\nL48: ##### Metadata\nL49: \nL50: Available format(s)\nL51:     【17†PDF】\nL52: Category\nL53:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL54: Publication info\nL55:     Published elsewhere. Major revision. USENIX Security 2024\nL56: Keywords\nL57:     【19†Zero-knowledge proofs】【20†ZKML】\nL58: Contact author(s)\nL59